          "create_time": 1793153714.193,
          "update_time": 1793153714.194,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Please read the manuscript in /papers/investigations-5/05_radial_theta_collision_geometry/, and 05_radial_theta_collision_geometry/notes/19_RADIAL_DEFORMATION_AND_ZERO_DYNAMICS_CONTINUATION_20261027.md. \n\nBefore proceeding, I'd like to mention a new idea that I was thinking about regarding the possible contradiction. I'm wondering if one approach to the problem is the following. Assume there is a collision at positive time. Can we find a deformation of the equations that leads to some form of dynamics that is clearly not possible? Perhaps there is a way to find a deformation that \"localizes\" that collision in some way and exposes the most salient features of it, which could potentially lead to more powerful tools for studying it (maybe estimating certain integrals in three dimensions instead of one, with more natural boundaries). \n\nPlease include this note in the brainstorm folder, and record chat history in the chat-histories folder.\n"
            ]
          },
